#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bonsai — a little Treemarks.

One Mac, a personal Claude and/or ChatGPT login, and one or more institution seats
(say, Stanford's Claude and Codex). Bonsai keeps every login as its own saved CLI
sign-in (a "pool"), shows how much room each one has left and when it resets, shows
this machine's load on the row beneath, and routes a job to the right login:

    bonsai doctor                       what is installed, what is signed in
    bonsai login <email> --claude       sign a login in as its own pool (also --codex)
    bonsai login <email> --codex --institution
    bonsai gauge                        the live board (q or Ctrl-C to quit); --once for one frame
    bonsai route [claude|codex]         the order a job would take, and why
    bonsai run --pool auto -- <cmd>     run any command under the chosen login
    bonsai job stanford-codex "prompt"  a headless job on a named seat
    bonsai job astra -f notes.md "..."  a model alias picks the provider; auto picks the login
    bonsai pass astra -f draft.md       an adversarial pass: a second model tries to break it
    bonsai jobs                         recent jobs and their outcomes
    bonsai install-skill                teach Claude Code the words ("route this to my stanford codex")

Pools live where the Treemarks fleet keeps them, so the two tools can share a machine:
~/.claude-pools/<email> (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR) and ~/.codex-pools/<email> (CODEX_HOME);
`default` is ~/.claude and ~/.codex, the logins you use by hand. Bonsai reads each
provider's own usage surface, the one the CLIs' /usage screens read. Those surfaces
are not documented APIs: Bonsai asks each login at most once per interval, backs off
on a 429, and treats every failure as "unknown", never as "empty". Tokens are never
printed, logged or written anywhere but where the CLIs already keep them.

Standard library only; macOS; Python 3.9 (the one Xcode's tools install) or newer.
"""
